  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Fair trade for African Blackwood: achieving self-sufficiency - phase 2</narrative>
  </title>
  <description>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Rare hardwoods such as African blackwood are uniquely suited to making musical instruments.  However currently communities living in Tanzanian forests containing blackwood receive less than 1% of its value and average annual household incomes are below US$200. The real value is captured by sawmills and musical instrument manufacturers.  Since 2004 MCDI, a Tanzanian NGO, has been working with villages in southern Tanzania to collectively manage and certify the forests that surround them and sell their wood for fair prices.  This grant will support 8 villages to maintain their current certifications and expand activities to 12 new villages.   It will support these villages to increase their income from timber by up to 1000 times and to reinvest the revenue to ensure on going income from their forests.   The project will be co-funded by a new ethical trading company, Sound &amp; Fair Ltd (S&amp;F).   This trading company will ensure there is a sufficient supply of processed, certified wood to meet growing manufacturer demand and pass very high margins which are currently captured by sawmills down to villagers. This is as an exit grant within which the villages will increase their income to a level where they can purchase support needed from a service provider using their own income.</narrative>
  </description>
